// DESIGN NOTE (Pyxis, 2026-07-05): this is the IPDL leg described in
// PyxisChannel.h/.cpp's "STILL NOT SOLVED HERE" notices (see the large
// comment blocks there for full background). Filling in the previously
// commented-out `if (XRE_IsContentProcess())` branch in
// PyxisChannel::OpenContentStream (and BeginAsyncRead -- see PyxisChannel.h)
// required deciding where this protocol should be managed. Two real
// precedents were read in full before choosing:
//
//  - netwerk/protocol/gio/PGIOChannel.ipdl: `manager PNecko`, a full
//    remoted-nsIChildChannel actor (OnStartRequest/OnDataAvailable/
//    OnStopRequest all forwarded over IPC, listener-diversion machinery,
//    etc). This is the right shape when the *entire* channel needs to be a
//    remote proxy of a parent-side channel (redirects, upload streams,
//    suspend/resume across the wire, ...). PyxisChannel doesn't need any of
//    that -- it's a single nsBaseChannel::OpenContentStream/BeginAsyncRead
//    call that wants one stream back for one already-fully-specified
//    request. Modeling it as a full remoted channel actor would duplicate a
//    lot of machinery for no benefit.
//  - netwerk/protocol/res/ExtensionProtocolHandler.{h,cpp}'s
//    SubstituteRemoteFileChannel/NewFD + the PNecko-level
//    GetExtensionStream/GetExtensionFD request/reply methods (see
//    netwerk/ipc/PNecko.ipdl, netwerk/ipc/NeckoParent.cpp
//    RecvGetExtensionStream/RecvGetExtensionFD): a single async
//    "resolve+open, hand back a stream (or FD)" request/reply pair. This is
//    the actually-matching shape for Pyxis. The one thing NOT copied
//    verbatim from this precedent is *where* the methods live: moz-extension
//    added them directly to the shared netwerk/ipc/PNecko.ipdl (and
//    NeckoParent/NeckoChild), which was reasonable there (moz-extension:'s
//    handler is core Necko) but would mean editing shared NeckoParent.h/.cpp
//    + NeckoChild.h/.cpp for Pyxis, a much wider blast radius than this
//    feature needs and explicitly out of scope per this task's constraints
//    (kept to PyxisChannel.{h,cpp}, this new IPDL/actor code, and moz.build).
//
// The chosen middle ground: a small, Pyxis-owned protocol, `manager
// PBackground` instead of `manager PNecko`. PBackground is the standard,
// already-real mechanism for "a content process needs to ask the parent for
// some self-contained async service" without hanging anything off PContent
// or PNecko's per-tab/per-channel actor trees -- see e.g.
// dom/locks/PLockManager.ipdl (`manager PBackground`, parent-side `Query()
// returns (...)`, constructor takes real args) and
// netwerk/protocol/http/PHttpBackgroundChannel.ipdl (same `manager
// PBackground` pattern, and notably ALSO namespace mozilla::net, i.e. a
// netwerk-owned protocol reusing the PBackground manager rather than
// PNecko). Adding a PBackground managee is still a small, unavoidable touch
// to shared files (ipc/glue/PBackground.ipdl's `include protocol
// PPyxisChannel;`/`manages PPyxisChannel;`/`async PPyxisChannel();` trio, and
// ipc/glue/BackgroundParentImpl.h/.cpp's Alloc/Recv overrides) -- there is no
// way to add ANY new manageable top-level-reachable protocol without
// registering it with *some* manager -- but it is meaningfully smaller than
// PNecko's channel-actor boilerplate (no Dealloc override needed here since
// this protocol doesn't request [ManualDealloc]; IPDL generates the trivial
// default) and does not touch NeckoParent/NeckoChild at all.
//
// Shape of the actual request: PyxisChannelChild is constructed (one per
// PyxisChannel::BeginAsyncRead call, i.e. one per content-process pyxis://
// load) and immediately sends GetContent(appId, path); the parent-side
// PyxisChannelParent (see PyxisChannelParent.h/.cpp) does the real
// PyxisOriginMap::Resolve + path confinement + open (via the shared
// PyxisChannelUtils, factored out of PyxisChannel.cpp specifically so this
// parent-side actor and the parent-process-direct PyxisChannel::
// OpenContentStream path share one implementation) and replies with either a
// stream + metadata or a failure nsresult. nsIInputStream is used directly
// (not wrapped in IPCStream) per ipc/glue/IPCStream.ipdlh's own comment:
// "If you don't need to handle nsIInputStream serialization failure,
// nsIInputStream may be used directly by IPDL protocols" -- Pyxis doesn't
// need the extra Maybe<IPCStream>-style failure handling IPCStream exists
// for, since a resolve/open failure is already communicated via the
// `nsresult status` half of the tuple; nsIInputStream here is only ever
// meaningful when status is NS_OK. This exactly matches how PNecko's own
// `returns (nullable nsIInputStream stream)` for GetExtensionStream is
// declared.

include protocol PBackground;

// Re-declaration of the same `using` already present in
// netwerk/ipc/PNecko.ipdl (and dom/ipc/DOMTypes.ipdlh) -- confirmed safe/
// intentional by reading ipc/ipdl/ipdl/type.py's visitUsingStmt: a `using`
// for a type already declared elsewhere is accepted (and reuses the existing
// symbol) as long as the [RefCounted]/[MoveOnly] attributes match exactly,
// which they do here. Each standalone top-level .ipdl file that references
// nsIInputStream directly needs its own `using` line; there's no implicit
// visibility from PNecko.ipdl just because both end up compiled into the
// same binary.
[RefCounted] using class nsIInputStream from "mozilla/ipc/IPCStreamUtils.h";

namespace mozilla {
namespace net {

// No [ChildImpl=virtual]/[ParentImpl=virtual] here, matching the other
// PBackground-managed protocols with hand-written final actor classes (e.g.
// dom/locks/PLockManager.ipdl) rather than PNecko's channel-actor style
// (e.g. netwerk/protocol/gio/PGIOChannel.ipdl, netwerk/ipc/PFileChannel.ipdl)
// which do use it. PyxisChannelChild/PyxisChannelParent below are final
// classes directly implementing the generated PPyxisChannelChild/
// PPyxisChannelParent base, not virtual-dispatch shims over a separate impl
// class, so the attribute isn't needed.
async protocol PPyxisChannel
{
  manager PBackground;

parent:
  // aAppId/aPath are the pyxis:// URI's raw host and filePath components
  // (filePath still has its leading '/' at this point) -- see
  // PyxisChannelChild::StartRequest for exactly how they're extracted from
  // the URI. The leading-slash-stripping (and all other confinement logic)
  // happens once, on the parent side, inside
  // PyxisChannelUtils::ResolveAndConfinePath -- exactly the same code
  // PyxisChannel::OpenContentStream's parent-direct path calls, per this
  // file's design note above.
  //
  // On success: aStatus == NS_OK, aStream is a valid, unread input stream
  // positioned at the start of the file, aMimeType is the inferred
  // Content-Type, aContentLength is the file size in bytes.
  //
  // On failure (unknown app-id, path escapes webroot, file missing, I/O
  // error, ...): aStatus is the specific failure code (matching exactly what
  // PyxisChannel::OpenContentStream would have returned for the same
  // request in the parent-direct path -- see PyxisChannelUtils.h), aStream
  // is null, aMimeType/aContentLength are unspecified and must be ignored.
  //
  // CORRECTION (2026-07-05, found via a real failed build, not by
  // inspection): aStream must be declared `nullable` -- without it, IPDL
  // codegen wraps a `[RefCounted] using`-declared refcounted type in
  // NotNull<> on both the resolver-construction side (parent:
  // NotNull<nsIInputStream*>) and the deserialization side (child:
  // NotNull<RefPtr<nsIInputStream>>), which cannot represent the "no
  // stream, this request failed" case a plain nullptr/null RefPtr needs to
  // express. Confirmed against the real precedent this design is modeled
  // on: netwerk/ipc/PNecko.ipdl's `GetExtensionStream(nullable nsIURI uri)
  // returns (nullable nsIInputStream stream)` -- its child-side consumer
  // (ExtensionProtocolHandler.cpp) resolves it as a plain `const
  // RefPtr<nsIInputStream>&`, never NotNull-wrapped, precisely because of
  // this keyword.
  async GetContent(nsCString aAppId, nsCString aPath)
      returns (nsresult aStatus, nullable nsIInputStream aStream,
               nsCString aMimeType, int64_t aContentLength);

  async __delete__();
};

}  // namespace net
}  // namespace mozilla
